= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

At <even integers> $n=2k$, the terms are $1+1/(2k)\to1$. At <odd integers> they are $-1-1/(2k-1)\to-1$. The largest eventual values therefore come from the subsequence at <even integers>, and
$$
\boxed{\limsup_{n\to\infty}(-1)^n(1+1/n)=1.}
$$
Explicitly, every sufficiently late term is below $1+\varepsilon$, while infinitely many <even integers> give terms greater than $1-\varepsilon$.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The <sine> has values $1,0,-1,0$ on the four residue classes of $n$ modulo four. For $n=4k+1$, the <exponential function> gives $b_n=e^{1-1/n}\to e$; the other residue classes tend to $1,e^{-1},1$. All terms satisfy $b_n\leq e$, and the first class approaches $e$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\limsup_{n\to\infty}e^{(1-1/n)\sin(n\pi/2)}=e.}
$$

= 2
{parent=Mathematics II}
{scope}

= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

A <series> $\sum_{n=1}^\infty a_n$ converges when its <partial sums> $s_N=\sum_{n=1}^N a_n$ have a finite <limit of a sequence>.

The <comparison test> says that if $0\leq a_n\leq b_n$ eventually and $\sum b_n$ converges, then $\sum a_n$ converges. Indeed, the <partial sums> of the nonnegative terms $a_n$ are increasing, and are bounded above by a fixed initial contribution plus the sum of the comparison <series>. The <bounded monotone sequence theorem> proves their convergence. Finite initial terms do not affect convergence. Applying this result to $|a_n|$ also proves <absolute convergence> whenever $|a_n|\leq b_n$ eventually with $\sum b_n<\infty$.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

**The implication is true, with <absolute convergence>.** Since $n^{3/2}a_n\to0$, there is $N$ such that $|a_n|\leq n^{-3/2}$ for $n\geq N$. The <p-series> $\sum n^{-3/2}$ converges because its exponent exceeds one. The <comparison test> proves $\sum|a_n|<\infty$, and <absolute convergence> implies convergence of $\sum a_n$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**The implication is false.** Set
$$
a_n=\frac1{n\log(n+1)}.
$$
Then $na_n=1/\log(n+1)\to0$. For $n\geq2$, $\log(n+1)\leq\log(2n)\leq2\log n$, so $a_n\geq1/(2n\log n)$. The <integral test> gives
$$
\int_2^R\frac{dx}{x\log x}=\log\log R-\log\log2\longrightarrow\infty.
$$
Thus the <comparison test> shows that $\sum a_n$ diverges.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The <binomial theorem> gives $\binom{2n}{n}\leq\sum_{k=0}^{2n}\binom{2n}{k}=4^n$. Therefore
$$
\left|\binom{2n}{n}3^{-2n}\sin n\right|\leq(4/9)^n.
$$
The <geometric series> with ratio $4/9$ converges. By the <comparison test>, the given <series> is **absolutely convergent**.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For $t\geq0$, $|\sin t|\leq t$, so
$$
0\leq\frac{n\sin^2(1/n)}{(\log n)^2}\leq\frac1{n(\log n)^2}.
$$
The <integral test> applies to the decreasing positive comparison function, and
$$
\int_2^\infty\frac{dx}{x(\log x)^2}=\frac1{\log2}<\infty.
$$
The <comparison test> proves that the <series> **converges**; since all terms are nonnegative, this is also <absolute convergence>.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Let $u(x)=(\log x)x^{-2/3}$. Its <derivative> is
$$
u'(x)=x^{-5/3}\left(1-\frac23\log x\right),
$$
which is negative for $x>e^{3/2}$. Also $u(x)\to0$, since a positive power eventually dominates the <natural logarithm>. Thus the <alternating series test> proves convergence of $\sum(-1)^n u(n)$.

For $n\geq3$, $u(n)\geq n^{-2/3}$, whose <p-series> diverges. The <comparison test> therefore rules out <absolute convergence>. The answer is **conditional convergence**.

= 3
{parent=Mathematics II}
{scope}

= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

For a <power series> $\sum a_nz^n$, its <radius of convergence> $R\in[0,\infty]$ separates <absolute convergence> for $|z|<R$ from divergence for $|z|>R$. One may define $R=\sup\{r\geq0:\sum|a_n|r^n<\infty\}$, allowing $R=\infty$.

To justify convergence inside the radius, choose $|z|<r<R$ with $\sum|a_n|r^n<\infty$. In particular $|a_n|r^n\leq M$ for some $M$, so
$$
|a_nz^n|\leq M(|z|/r)^n.
$$
The <comparison test> with a <geometric series> proves <absolute convergence>. Conversely, convergence at a point of modulus $\rho$ makes $a_nz^n$ bounded there and gives <absolute convergence> at all smaller moduli by the same estimate. Consequently a convergent point of modulus greater than $R$ would contradict the definition of $R$.

For $0<R<\infty$, the real <domain of convergence> is one of $(-R,R)$, $[-R,R)$, $(-R,R]$, or $[-R,R]$. At $R=1$ these are realized respectively by
$$
\sum z^n,\qquad \sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{z^n}{n},\qquad \sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^nz^n}{n},\qquad \sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{z^n}{n^2}.
$$
The endpoint assertions follow from the <harmonic series>, <alternating series test>, and <p-series>. Replacing $z$ by $z/R$ realizes every finite positive radius. The degenerate domain $\{0\}$ occurs for $\sum n!z^n$, while the entire <real line> occurs for $\sum z^n/n!$. Their radii are zero and infinity by the <ratio test>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The <Cauchy-Hadamard theorem> states $R^{-1}=\limsup|a_n|^{1/n}$. At the <prime numbers> $n=p$, this root equals $p^{2/p}\to1$. There are infinitely many <prime numbers>, so these indices form an unbounded <subsequence>. For every other positive index the root is zero. The <limit superior> is therefore one, and $\boxed{R=1}$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

By <Stirling's approximation>,
$$
(n!)^{1/n}\sim\frac ne(2\pi n)^{1/(2n)},\qquad
|a_n|^{1/n}=\frac{n^{1+1/n^2}}{(n!)^{1/n}}\longrightarrow e.
$$
Here $(2\pi n)^{1/(2n)}\to1$ and $n^{1/n^2}\to1$, as is seen by taking the <natural logarithm>. The <Cauchy-Hadamard theorem> gives $\boxed{R=e^{-1}}$.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

At the nonzero indices $n=m!$ with $m\geq2$,
$$
|a_{m!}|^{1/(m!)}=10^{m/(m!)}\longrightarrow1,
$$
since $m/(m!)=1/(m-1)!\to0$. At all other sufficiently large indices the coefficient is zero. The initial coefficients do not affect the <limit superior>, which equals one. The <Cauchy-Hadamard theorem> therefore gives $\boxed{R=1}$.

