= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The <mean value theorem> states that if $f:[a,b]\to\mathbb R$ is <continuous> on $[a,b]$ and <differentiable> on $(a,b)$, where $a<b$, there is $c\in(a,b)$ such that
$$
\boxed{f(b)-f(a)=f'(c)(b-a).}
$$
No <continuity> assumption on the <derivative> is needed.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The <constancy theorem> states that a <differentiable function> on an <interval> whose <derivative> vanishes everywhere is <constant>. At any included endpoints, <continuity> there is understood.

Indeed, take arbitrary $x<y$ in the <interval>. The <mean value theorem> applies on $[x,y]$ and gives $f(y)-f(x)=f'(c)(y-x)=0$. Every pair of values is equal, proving the assertion. The <interval> hypothesis ensures that the whole segment $[x,y]$ lies in the <domain>.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The <derivative> of $h(x)=f(x)-\sin x$ vanishes. By the <constancy theorem> on the <interval> $I$,
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\sin x+C\quad(x\in I).}
$$
**This need not hold on a disconnected <domain>.** On $I=(-\infty,0)\cup(0,\infty)$, define $f(x)=\sin x$ for $x<0$ and $f(x)=\sin x+1$ for $x>0$. The <function> is <differentiable> throughout $I$ with $f'(x)=\cos x$, but no single $C$ works on both components.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Suppose distinct <fixed points> $x<y$ existed. Then $h(t)=f(t)-t$ satisfies $h(x)=h(y)=0$. <Rolle's theorem> supplies $c\in(x,y)$ with $h'(c)=0$, hence $f'(c)=1$, contrary to the hypothesis. Thus **there is at most one <fixed point>**. The argument does not require $f'$ to be <continuous>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For every real $t$, $f(t)-t=1/(1+e^t)>0$, so **there is no <fixed point>**. Direct <differentiation> gives
$$
f'(t)=1-\frac{e^t}{(1+e^t)^2}.
$$
Writing $u=e^t>0$, the inequality $(1+u)^2\geq4u$ shows
$$
\boxed{\frac34\leq f'(t)<1.}
$$
In particular $0<f'(t)<1$, but $f'(t)\to1$ as $t\to\pm\infty$. There is no common <contraction constant> smaller than one. Strict pointwise bounds on the <derivative> therefore do not suffice for the <contraction mapping theorem>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

By the <mean value theorem>, $|f(x)-f(y)|\leq A|x-y|$ for all real $x,y$. Thus $f$ is a <contraction mapping> on the <complete metric space> $\mathbb R$. Starting from arbitrary $x_1$, define $x_{n+1}=f(x_n)$. Induction gives $|x_{n+1}-x_n|\leq A^{n-1}|x_2-x_1|$. For $m>n$, the <triangle inequality> and a <geometric series> yield
$$
|x_m-x_n|\leq\frac{A^{n-1}}{1-A}|x_2-x_1|\longrightarrow0.
$$
Hence $(x_n)$ is a <Cauchy sequence> and <completeness of the real numbers> supplies a <limit> $x$. The <Lipschitz continuity> of $f$ implies $f(x)=\lim f(x_n)=\lim x_{n+1}=x$. Thus the limit is a <fixed point>, independently of the initial value. If $y$ were another, $|x-y|\leq A|x-y|$ would force $x=y$. This proves the <contraction mapping theorem> in this setting, with error estimate
$$
\boxed{|x-x_n|\leq\frac{A^{n-1}}{1-A}|x_2-x_1|.}
$$

= 7
{parent=Mathematics II}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

Assume, as required for the displayed integrals, that the $f_n$ are <Riemann integrable> on $[a,b]$. Their <uniform limit> $f$ is also <Riemann integrable>: if $\sup|f-f_N|<\varepsilon$, the upper and lower <Darboux sums> for a <partition of an interval> differ for $f$ by at most the corresponding difference for $f_N$ plus $2\varepsilon(b-a)$. Choosing $N$ and then a sufficiently fine <partition of an interval> proves the <Riemann integrability criterion> for $f$.

The integral estimate then gives
$$
\left|\int_a^b f_n(x)\,dx-\int_a^b f(x)\,dx\right|
\leq(b-a)\sup_{x\in[a,b]}|f_n(x)-f(x)|\longrightarrow0.
$$
This proves that <uniform convergence preserves integrals on a compact interval>. Some integrability hypothesis is necessary: <uniform convergence> alone cannot make the integral of an arbitrary starting <function> exist.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Let $f_n$ be the <continuous> triangular bump
$$
f_n(x)=\begin{cases}
 n^2x,&0\leq x\leq1/n,\\
 n(2-nx),&1/n\leq x\leq2/n,\\
 0,&2/n\leq x\leq1,
\end{cases}\qquad n\geq2,
$$
and set $f_1=0$. At $x=0$ every term is zero, and each fixed $x>0$ eventually lies outside the support, so $f_n\to0$ <pointwise>. The triangle has base $2/n$ and height $n$, hence
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1 f_n(x)\,dx=1\quad(n\geq2),\qquad \int_0^1 0\,dx=0.}
$$
This <concentrating triangular bump> shows that <pointwise convergence> does not preserve integrals, even for <continuous functions> on a <compact interval>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Take $f_n(x)=x^n$ on $[0,1]$. Each <function> is <continuous>, and the <pointwise limit> is $f(x)=0$ for $0\leq x<1$, while $f(1)=1$. Nevertheless
$$
\boxed{\int_0^1 f_n(x)\,dx=\frac1{n+1}\longrightarrow0=\int_0^1 f(x)\,dx.}
$$
The limiting <function> is <Riemann integrable>, since changing a value at one point does not change its <Riemann integral>. There is no <uniform convergence>: for every $n$, $\sup_{[0,1]}|f_n-f|=1$, with the <supremum> approached as $x\uparrow1$ from below. Equivalently, the <uniform limit theorem> would require the limit to be <continuous>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

**No: the unbounded length of the <domain> matters.** Let $\phi(t)=\max\{1-|t|,0\}$ and define $f_n(x)=n^{-1}\phi(x/n)$ on $\mathbb R$. These are <continuous functions> with <compact support>, and $\sup_{\mathbb R}|f_n|=1/n\to0$, so the <sequence> has <uniform convergence> to zero. However, the <change of variables> $t=x/n$ gives
$$
\boxed{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f_n(x)\,dx=\int_{-1}^1(1-|t|)\,dt=1.}
$$
The integral of the limit is zero. The supports widen as the heights decrease; a uniform bound on the heights gives no bound on the total area over an infinite <interval>.

